\f0\fs24 \cf0 Final Presentation Notes\
\
Step 1)\
Assess the quality of the reads using Falco. \
Tool: Falco\
Version: Galaxy Version 1.3.0+galaxy0\
Parameters: Raw read data from your current history: Transcriptome reads; all other default.\
Files Downloaded: Forward and Reverse Falco reports for the same condition (FalcoSporangia3ForwardQC.html and FalcoSporangia3ReverseQC.html).\
\
Step 2)\
Remove portions of the reads that do not meet the quality control standards (ex. adapters, reads with a low quality score, etc.).\
Tool: fastp\
Version: Galaxy Version 1.3.3+galaxy0\
Parameters: Set to Paired Collection; Select paired collections: Transcriptome Reads; all other default.\
File Downloaded: Fastp report for the same condition as in the prior step (FastpSporangia3Report.html)\
\
Step 3)\
Align the sequence reads with the reference genome. \
Tool: HISAT2\
Version: Galaxy Version 2.2.2+galaxy0\
Parameters: Source for Reference Genome: Use a genome from history; Select the reference genome: bd.fna; Is this a single or paired library: Paired-end Dataset Collection; Paired Collection: Fastp output; all other default.\
File Downloaded: None\
\
Step 4)\
Assemble a transcript using the short read sequences.\
Tool: StringTie\
Version: Galaxy Version 2.2.3+galaxy0\
Parameters: Input short mapped reads: HISAT2 output file; Use a reference file to guide assembly?: Use reference GTF/GFF3; Reference File: Use a file from history; GTF/GFF3 dataset to guide assembly: bd.gff; all other default.\
File Downloaded: None\
\
Step 5)\
Assemble the transcripts resulting from the StringTie tool into one.\
Tool: StringTie Merge\
Version: Galaxy Version 2.2.3+galaxy0\
Parameters: Transcripts: StringTie output; Reference annotation to include in the merging: bd.gff\
File Downloaded: None\
\
Step 6)\
Determine the number of occurrences transcripts appear in the dataset.\
Tool: featureCounts\
Version: Galaxy Version 2.1.1+galaxy0\
Parameters: Alignment file: HISAT2 output; Genome annotation file: A GFF/GTF file in your history; Gene annotation file: StringTie Merge output; Gff gene identifier: transcript_id; Does the input have read pairs: Yes, paired-end and count them as 1 single fragment; all other default.\
File Downloaded: None\
\
Step 7)\
Determine if genes are up- or down-regulated by comparing both conditions and their replicates. \
Tool: DESeq2\
Version: Galaxy Version 2.11.40.8+galaxy2\
Parameters: 1: Factor - Specify a factor name: Reproducive_Component; 1: Factor level - Specify a factor level: Zoospore; Counts file(s): all under the zoospore condition; 2: Factor level - Specify a factor level: Sporangia; Counts file(s): all under the sporangia condition; Use beta priors: True; Output options: select both \'93Generate plots for visualizing the analysis results\'94 and \'93Output normalized counts\'94; all other default.\
File Downloaded: DESeq2Plots.pdf, NormalizedCounts.tabular\
\
Step 8)\
Filter to keep all datasets where the P-adjusted value that is less than 0.05.\
Tool: Filter (data on any column using simple expressions)\
Version: Galaxy Version 1.1.1\
Parameters: Filter: DESeq2 output file; With the following condition: c7<0.05; all other default.\
File Downloaded: None\
\
Step 9)\
Filter to keep all datasets with a log2(FC) that has an absolute value that is greater than one.\
Tool: Filter (data on any column using simple expressions)\
Version: Galaxy Version 1.1.1\
Parameters: Filter: Output of prior filtering step; With the following condition: abs(c3)>1; all other default.\
File Downloaded: GenesFilteredPandLog2FC.tabular\
\
Step 10)\
Create headers for the filtered dataset.\
Tool: TextEdit\
Version: 1.20 (415)\
Parameters: In a blank doc, add the following, being sure that tab is used to separate (without spaces): GeneID / Base mean / log2(FC) / StdErr / Wald-Stats / P-value / P-adj. Once complete, select \'93Upload\'94 and go to \'93Paste/Fetch data\'94. Copy and paste the text from the TextEdit document into this textbox, name it \'93header\'94. Be sure to select \'93tabular\'94, and \'93Start\'94 to begin the upload.\
\
Step 11)\
Add the uploaded header to the filtered dataset.\
Tool: Concatenate multiple datasets or collection\
Version: Galaxy Version 1.0.0\
Parameters: Concatenate Dataset: header; select Insert Dataset: input the latest filtered dataset from step 9; all other default. Was renamed to \'93Differential Gene Expression of Zoospore and Sporangia\'94.\
File Downloaded: None\
\
Step 12)\
Visualize the up- and down-regulation of genes using a volcano plot.\
Tool: Volcano Plot\
Version: Galaxy Version 4.0.3+galaxy0\
Parameters: Specify an input file: \'93Differential Gene Expression of Zoospore and Sporangia\'94 (from prior step); FDR (adjusted P value) column number: Column 7; P value (raw) column number: Column 6; Log Fold Change column number: Column 3; Labels column number: Column 1; all other default.\
File Downloaded: VolcanoPlot.pdf\
\
Step 13)\
Delete all genes except for the top 10 up- and down-regulated genes.\
Tool: Excel\
Version: 16.108.1\
Parameters: Sort the data by the log scale fold change. Delete all rows except for the top 10 up-regulated and the top 10 down-regulated genes. Save as a .csv file. Upload to Galaxy and convert to tabular by editing the attributes, going to the \'93datatypes tab\'94, and selecting \'93tabular\'94 instead of \'93text\'94.\
File Downloaded: Top10UpandDown.tabular (later uploaded to galaxy, see parameters for this step).\
\
Step 14)\
Combine the top 10 up- and down-regulated genes file with the normalized counts file (should result in having the stats, log change, P value, and counts for each gene).\
Tool: Join two datasets (side by side in a specific field)\
Version: Galaxy Version 2.1.3\
Parameters: Join: Top 10 Up- and Down-regulated genes file; Using column: Column 1; With: Normalized counts file; and column: Column 1; Keep the header lines: true; all other default.\
File Downloaded: None\
\
Step 15)\
Remove data except for the gene name and the zoospore/sporangia replicates (keep columns 8-14).\
Tool: Cut (columns from a table)\
Version: Galaxy Version 1.0.2\
Parameters: Cut columns: c8-c14; From: Output of Join two datasets (from prior step); all other default.\
File Downloaded: None\
\
Step 16)\
Visualize the expression levels of the genes with a heat map.\
Tool: Heatmap2\
Version: Galaxy Version 3.3.0+galaxy0\
Parameters: Input: Resulting file from step 15 (after using the cut tool); Data transformation: Log2(value+1) transform my data; colormap pattern can vary (mine was blues); all other default.\
File Downloaded: The resulting file \'93Heatmap2.pdf\'94.\
\
Step 17)\
Filter to keep coding sequences in the annotation file (want only the genes that are labeled CDS).\
Tool: Filter (data on any column using simple expressions)\
Version: Galaxy Version 1.1.1\
Parameters: Filter: bd.gff; With the following condition: c3==\'91CDS\'92; all other default.\
File Downloaded: None\
\
Step 18)\
Remove all columns except for the column listing the genes attributes (column 9).\
Tool: Cut (columns from a table)\
Version: Galaxy Version 1.0.2\
Parameters: Cut columns: c9; From: output from filtering in the prior step; all other default.\
File Downloaded: None\
\
Step 19)\
Separate the data from one column into multiple columns wherever there is a semicolons.  \
Tool: Convert (delimiters to TAB)\
Version: Galaxy Version 1.0.1\
Parameters: Convert all: Semicolons; in Dataset: Output from the cutting in the prior step; all other default.\
File Downloaded: None\
\
Step 20)\
In column two, remove the beginning part of the name (\'93Parent=\'93).\
Tool: Replace Text (in a specific column)\
Version: Galaxy Version 9.5+galaxy3\
Parameters: File to process: Output of the convert tool in prior step; in column: Column2; Find pattern: Parent=; all other default.\
File Downloaded: None\
\
Step 21)\
Remove all unneeded data, leaving only the gene name (column 2) and the description of the gene product (column 8).\
Tool: Cut (columns from a table)\
Version: Galaxy Version 1.0.2\
Parameters: Cut columns: c2,c8; From: Output from the replace text tool from the prior step; all other default.\
File Downloaded: None\
\
Step 22)\
Combine the dataset that has the gene name in addition to the product description (output of step 21) with the dataset that contains the gene statistics in the top 10 list (output of step 13).\
Tool: Join two Datasets (side by side on a specified field)\
Version: Galaxy Version 2.1.3\
Parameters: Join: Output of cutting in the prior step; using column: Column 1; with: the top 10 up and down file; and column: 1; Keep the header lines: Yes; all other default.\
File Downloaded: GeneProductsTop10UpandDown.tabular\
Note: The original file has duplicated results listed in the file. These duplicates were removed using Excel.}